    49	
    50	## 1. Forced host work in every GDN prefill
    51	
    52	### Causal chain
    53	
    54	1. `adapter:219–242` computes `logical_len = physical_len` when the caller omits `valid_len`, then **always** forwards `valid_len=logical_len`.
    55	2. `q36/layer.py:291–295` selects GDN `mode="chunk"`; `q36/gdn/decode.py:28–79` forwards the length, persistent recurrent/conv state, cached taps, and mega-fused projection weight.
    56	3. The weight loader constructs that mega weight and taps (`q36/gdn/weights.py:215–247`). Therefore the ordinary prefill path is `experimental/ttnn_gated_deltanet.py:415–459`: mega projection, then `_causal_conv1d_fir` with a non-None length.
    57	4. FIR lines 181–203 allocate `torch.zeros(B,3,T+3)`, fill one-hot indices on the host, and call `ttnn.from_torch(..., device=device)` on **every invocation**. The device matmul selects the state tail; the activation itself is not downloaded to Torch.
    58	5. For actual padding, `experimental/ttnn_delta_rule_seq.py:439–457` separately constructs and uploads a `[B*32,T,1]` Torch mask. This second path is skipped for scalar `valid_len == T`, unlike FIR.
    59	
    60	Concrete cases at B=1:
    61	
    62	| Logical length | Physical length | FIR selector upload | Scan mask upload |
    63	|---:|---:|---|---|
    64	| 1 | 128 | yes | yes |
    65	| 127 | 128 | yes | yes |
    66	| 128 | 128 | yes | no |
    67	| 129 | 256 | yes | yes |
    68	| 2200 | 2304 | yes | yes |
    69	| 262144 | 262144 | yes | no |
    70	
    71	This is runtime host tensor preparation/upload, not CPU execution of the GDN recurrence. That distinction does not make it satisfy the requested device-only forward. The mesh command queue explicitly rejects writes during capture (`tt_metal/distributed/fd_mesh_command_queue.cpp:616,655,804`). The sibling TP implementation documents the same failure and deliberately preserves `valid_len=None` on full chunks (`q36/gdn/tp.py:558–564`). This contrast supports the diagnosis without relying on its comments as proof.
    72	
    73	### Smallest local repair
    74	
    75	- Preserve `None` for a wholly valid internal chunk. FIR's static tail slice at lines 176–180 selects exactly the same three rows as the one-hot for `valid_len=T`.
   230	- Blackhole's per-core L1 size is 1536 KiB (`tt_metal/hw/inc/internal/tt-1xx/blackhole/dev_mem_map.h:33`). A device-wide 4 GiB L1 activation is not a viable native-context execution plan. The exact earliest allocation failure was not measured.
   231	
   232	**Repair:** the autoport owns bounded outer chunks independently of native context capacity. Keep global positions and caller-owned paged K/V at the full supported context, advance GDN state through each real chunk, and stage only bounded activations in L1. Do not cap context to 128/2048 merely because a kernel uses that chunk size. For full attention, align bulk chunks with page boundaries and handle arbitrary continuation/tails explicitly as described above.
   233	
   234	## 7. Inherited precision is a policy choice, not established accuracy
   235	
   236	| Component | Reached policy | Evidence |
   237	|---|---|---|
   238	| GDN projections, including mega projection and output | BFP8; LoFi, FP32 destination accumulation | `q36/gdn/weights.py:77–88,115–122,208–228`; `gated_deltanet.py:37–46` |
   239	| Full-attention Q/K/V/O weights | BFP8; LoFi projection compute | `q36/attention/weights.py:18–27`; `gated_attention.py:26–35` |
   240	| Routed expert gate/up | BFP4 | `q36/moe/weights.py:52–58,108–125` |
   241	| Routed expert down | BFP8 | Same loader, lines 128–136 |
   242	| Shared expert gate/up and down | BFP4 and BFP8; LoFi | `q36/moe/shared.py:35–37`; `q36/mlp.py:143–158,187–195` |
   243	| Persistent GDN state | BF16 at construction/copyback; ordinary decode `high_precision=False` | `q36/gdn/gated_deltanet.py:50–65`; `experimental/ttnn_delta_rule_ops.py:484–508` |
   244	| Chunk preprocessing / scan | Float32 tensors; preprocessing HiFi4, scan descriptor HiFi2 | `seq.py:413–419`; scan factory lines 174–183 |
   245	| Router | BF16 weight; HiFi2/FP32 accumulation, but no requested float32 probability output | `q36/moe/router.py:20–55` |
   246	
   247	For BF16 hidden input, router matmul defaults its **output** to the activation dtype, and softmax's output spec retains that dtype (`ops/matmul/device/matmul_device_operation.cpp:2980–2983`; `ops/normalization/softmax/device/softmax_device_operation.cpp:350–363`). FP32 accumulation is not FP32 stored probabilities. HF computes softmax/top-k normalization in float32 before converting the selected weights back (HF:784–790). BF16 probabilities can introduce ties near the eighth/ninth expert boundary; actual route changes on these weights were not measured. GDN prefill can have different intermediate dtypes, so this is not a claim that every router invocation is necessarily BF16.
   248	
   249	Setting `QWEN_GDN_FP32_STATE=1` alone is insufficient for this single-device class: it changes the temporary state returned by the seq adapter (`seq.py:199–203`), while persistent storage remains BF16 and copyback targets that original storage. High precision must be an end-to-end local policy, including state allocation and update computation.
   250	
   251	**Repair:** expose an autoport-local functional precision policy. Load a BF16 weight baseline directly from checkpoint tensors, use appropriate high-fidelity/FP32 accumulation, preserve FP32 recurrence and decay where needed to match HF, and use float32 routing probabilities through selection/normalization. Cast at explicit module boundaries. Do not upcast already quantized BFP4/BFP8 weights and call that a BF16 baseline. Namespace cached tensors by model identity, precision, and layout when changing policy. Measure the whole real decoder and component deltas before retaining any lower precision.
   252	
   253	No evidence here proves that BFP4 alone violates a particular PCC threshold. The definite issue is inheriting it without a baseline or exposed policy; the diagonal-damped recurrence is a stronger, independently proven semantic discrepancy.
   254	
   255	## State, trace, and completion ledger
   256	
   257	| Boundary | Current behavior | Consequence / required invariant |
   258	|---|---|---|
   259	| GDN construction | Allocates zero recurrent and three-token conv buffers once | Good B=1 identity/address ownership; wrong capacity for B>1. |
   260	| GDN prefill | Reads previous conv/recurrent buffers, derives real tail, copies final state into original buffers | Continuation exists; do not replace it with zero state on every chunk. Fix masking, recurrence policy, and batching while preserving this behavior. |
   261	| GDN T=1 decode | Conv and recurrent helpers copy into persistent buffers | Suitable source structure for trace-stable state; hardware trace support still needs a complete replay test. |
   262	| GDN reset | Copies zero templates into the same allocations | Correct address-preserving pattern; generalize to selected slots. |
   263	| Full-attention binding | Stores external K/V objects | Callers own allocation and pages; trace captures their addresses. Rebinding requires a deliberate recapture/rebind protocol. |
   264	| Full-attention concat decode | Replaces Python `past_key/past_value` with growing tensors | A captured decode trace cannot rerun these Python assignments and dynamically grow caches. Use persistent paged caches for replay. |
   265	| Full-attention paged decode | Reads device position/table and updates bound cache | B=1 source path avoids runtime Torch conversion; ensure persistent inputs/tables/positions and correct B axes. |
   266	| Layer failure after attention | MoE runs after attention cache/state mutation | A failed call is not transactional. Reset/reseed before retrying a rejected functional test. |
   267	
   268	No adapter forward/reset explicitly synchronizes the device. TTNN calls enqueue work; a host-side return or assertion is not a completed correctness check. Cache writers use NoC barriers before releasing consumed tiles, and the GDN scan communicates through explicit buffers, but those local barriers do not replace host-visible completion when validating results. Future tests must read outputs/state or synchronize after replay before claiming success.
   269	
   270	The selected seq kernel's dimensions do not suggest a C/K/V tile-count mismatch for this model. Instantiated at B=1,T=128, it uses BH=32, NC=1, Ct=Kt=Vt=4: six matrix input streams each contain 16 tiles, decay one tile, inverse four one-tile blocks, and initial/final state 16 tiles per head. T=256 changes NC to 2, not these per-chunk counts. Reader counts (`reader_gated_delta_attn.cpp:103–200`), forward-substitution consumers, and final-chunk state selection (`compute/gated_delta_attn.cpp:244–277,419–435`) agree at this level. This was a focused count/ownership check, not a proof that all low-level arithmetic or scheduling is bug-free. No supplied hang warrants promoting a speculative queue bug over the concrete caller/preprocessing violations.
   271	
   272	## Smallest coherent autoport-local repair plan
   273	
   274	1. **Own composition and contracts locally.** Keep correct checkpoint remapping and reusable kernels. Add explicit `[B,T,H]` validation, the MoE-only row/rank bridge, logical output slicing, and fail-fast metadata checks before state mutation. Avoid process-global monkeypatches to shared Qwen36 modules.
   275	2. **Own a functional precision/state policy.** Construct local weights/state with explicit dtypes. Reuse mathematical building blocks, not unproven low-precision constructor defaults. Preserve real Q/K/V dimensions, all 256 experts, top-8 routing, and the gated shared expert.
   276	3. **Make GDN forward entirely device-resident.** Use exact recurrence, device FIR/tail extraction, and persistent per-slot buffers. Full aligned chunks may pass `valid_len=None`; handle real tails with device recurrent steps or a device mask. Precompute constants before capture. Bounded groups can support batches beyond the seq kernel's one-head-per-core limit.
   277	4. **Give full attention one cache owner.** Use caller-owned persistent paged storage through prefill and decode. Derive/validate request table rows, logical positions, page capacity and dtype. Use page-aligned fills for bulk work and ordered exact-position updates/decode for arbitrary tails and starts. Never batch adjacent same-request token writes as independent tile owners.
   278	5. **Separate kernel chunk size from request length.** Accept logical lengths at the public interface, orchestrate bounded work, and keep native context metadata. Initially a slower per-token tail/unaligned path is sufficient if every operation stays on device and state/output semantics match HF.
   279	6. **Capture only after preparation.** Allocate stable input/output/state/cache and device position/table buffers, warm the selected paths, then capture. Replay with changed tensor contents, not replacement Python objects or shape growth. GDN state and full-attention positions must advance exactly once per token; request reset must preserve captured addresses.
   280	
   281	These are proposals, not implemented fixes. A two-line `valid_len=None` change would address only aligned FIR uploads and leave the other findings intact.
   282	
   283	## Validation needed after repair
   284	
   285	Existing autoport tests (`tests/test_functional_contract.py`) check config, padding arithmetic, checkpoint index keys, and shard selection; they do not construct or run a TT decoder. Existing single-device layer tests (`q36` tests/unit/test_layer.py) are shape/finite-value sanity checks. The standalone MoE test supplies rank-4 input directly (`tests/unit/test_moe.py:45–50`), which bypasses the faulty integration boundary.
   286	
   287	Use the actual pinned layer 0 and layer 3 checkpoint tensors and the installed HF layer with matching caches/RoPE. Validate output, selected routes where relevant, and post-call state/cache contents. Suggested matrix:
   288	
   289	| Dimension | Required contrasts |
   290	|---|---|
   291	| Length | 1, 2, 3, 31, 32, 33, 127, 128, 129, 255, 256, 2200; longer bounded runs through 262144 when physical capacity is established |
   292	| Continuation | Monolithic vs splits at 1/3/127/128/129 and page boundaries; prefill→prefill→decode; decode→continuation; fresh/reset equivalence |
   293	| Batch | B=1 and B=2 minimum; later admitted larger B; compare each row to an independent run; unequal lengths and selected-slot reset |
   294	| Paging | Permuted nonzero physical pages; nonzero request rows; page sizes 32/128; starts 0/128/160/129; guard values in other users' pages and unowned tails |
   295	| Dispatch | Bound cache with omitted chunk table; missing required full table/position; valid first-chunk default; reject incomplete metadata before mutation |
   296	| Trace | Multiple decode replays with changed hidden input, RoPE, positions, and table contents; compare to eager; reset/reseed in place; verify output and state after synchronization |
   297	| Host boundary | Instrument runtime forward helpers to reject Torch/from_torch/to_torch while allowing construction and test I/O; include padded and unpadded calls |
   298	| Precision | Baseline first; lower each policy independently; whole-decoder output and recurrent/cache comparisons, not merely finite values |
   299	
   300	No hardware acceptance, accuracy threshold pass, or performance claim can be inferred from this report's static checks.
   301	
   302	## Other Potential Issues and ruled-out suspects
   303	
   304	- **Convolution split constants:** `q36/weight_mapping.py:29–30,95–103` hardcodes Q=K=2048. Those values match this checkpoint; this is not a 9B-to-35B bug here. `FULL_ATTENTION_LAYERS` is not used by the remapping dispatch; actual layer kind comes from configuration.
   305	- **K−1 versus K conv history:** TT stores three previous tokens for a width-4 FIR; HF stores a width-4 window and prepends it before slicing continuation output. Three prior inputs are sufficient for the next causal output. The FIR real-tail window starts at `valid_len` in `[old_history(3),x]`, so even lengths 1 and 2 retain the required old history. Do not replace it with `x[max(0,L-3):L]` without the missing prefix.
   306	- **GDN continuation is not missing wholesale:** `q36/gdn/decode.py:54,70,82–90` passes and updates both persistent states. The faults are policy, lengths, batch capacity, and runtime host preparation.
   307	- **Repeated GDN heads:** q/k are repeated twice from 16 to 32 in the reached single-device path, matching HF. The seq adapter then sees Hq=H=32 and does not repeat them again.
   308	- **Norm conventions:** decoder and full-attention Q/K norms apply `1+weight`; GDN output norm uses the raw standard RMSNorm weight. Source ordering matches HF; numerical precision remains separate.
   309	- **Expert gate/up order:** checkpoint `[gate|up]` is deliberately repacked as `[up|gate]` for TTNN SwiGLU (`q36/moe/weights.py:139–142`), whose helper uses that order. This is not evidence of swapped activation/gate math. Top-k ordering is also harmless if expert IDs and weights remain paired; precision-induced selection changes are a different question.
   310	- **Decay clipping and output clipping:** seq preprocessing clamps exponent arguments to `[-20,0]` (`seq.py:539–554,638–653`); GDN clips its normalized gated output to ±1e4 (`ttnn_gated_deltanet.py:757`). HF does not apply those exact clamps. They are additional semantic/numerical deviations, but no inspected real activation proves they trigger materially here. Keep secondary to the always-active default diagonal change.
   311	- **Memory and precision promotion:** device casts, aliasing/deallocation, and BFP cache reload behavior need checking when implementing the local baseline. This report does not infer a use-after-free solely from an explicit `deallocate` after a TTNN operation.
   312	- **RoPE ownership:** the adapter accepts cos/sin but does not build them. The actual checkpoint rotates 64 of 256 dimensions with theta=10,000,000 and its configured multimodal sections; the generic helper derives rotary width from cos/sin. Do not reuse an incorrectly shaped default table. There is no supplied adapter caller proving a wrong table was used.
   313	- **TP sibling evidence is not this path:** the adapter explicitly admits one device and takes single-device constructors. TP fixes, batched state helpers, exact-KV updates, and newer fused GDN kernels do not automatically apply to it.
   314	
   315	## Report review
   316	
   317	After the first draft, recheck each headline against its exact reachable branch and lowered contract. In particular: preserve the distinction between prefill host uploads and decode capture; verify the public head-concat squeeze; do not attribute non-paged SDPA checks to the paged path; do not claim all padded cache writes corrupt real outputs; and do not equate low precision with a measured accuracy failure. The final review results are recorded below after that pass.

